<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Forest Bathing and Other Biohacks – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Managing Stress in Changing Times</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Forest Bathing and Other Biohacks</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Infer opinions in a simple presentation or lecture. Interactive: Participate in group discussions on various topics, including local and global interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can infer and discuss opinions expressed in simple lectures on environmental topics (e.g., "Visiting the Sendero Los Quetzales in Volcán Barú National Park helps people feel calmer and more relaxed due to the serene natural environment."). Can participate in group discussions on global topics by sharing opinions (e.g., "I think stress affects people's mental health by making them feel overwhelmed. Practicing mindfulness could really help reduce stress and improve focus."). Can actively participate in group discussions on local and global topics related to managing stress and mindfulness by asking clarifying questions to understand better others' opinions (e.g., "Why are more students practicing mindfulness these days?" "People are practicing mindfulness to stay calm during stressful times.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to activate prior knowledge and introduce key concepts and language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different natural environments (e.g., a serene forest, a calm beach, a vibrant park like Parque Omar) and also images of people looking stressed versus relaxed. Students are asked to briefly describe what they see and how the images make them feel. The teacher asks, "What do these images suggest about how we can manage stress?" "Do you think spending time in nature helps you relax?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary for the lesson using visual aids and gestures: <i>forest bathing, mindfulness, relaxation, stress, soothing, beneficial, overwhelmed</i>. The teacher models the pronunciation of words containing the /uː/ sound (e.g., "soothing," "smooth") and multisyllabic words with full and reduced vowels (e.g., "mindfulness" /ˈmaɪnd.fəl.nəs/, "beneficial" /ˌben.ɪˈfɪʃ.əl/), emphasizing clear enunciation. For example, "Listen: <i>soooothing</i>... repeat: <i>soothing</i>." "Listen: <i>mííínd-fəl-nəs</i>... repeat: <i>mindfulness</i>." The teacher uses these words in simple sentences to show context, e.g., "Forest bathing is a very <b>soothing</b> practice."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'forest bathing' about taking a shower in the woods? (No, it's about being immersed in nature.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'beneficial,' is it good or bad for you? (It's good for you; it helps.)"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language and content, connected to the scenario "Managing Stress in Changing Times" through authentic audio/visual materials.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short informative presentation (approximately 2-3 minutes) titled "Nature's Prescription: How Forest Bathing and Mindfulness Combat Stress." The presentation features a speaker sharing opinions on the benefits of these practices. It includes instances of gerunds ("Practicing mindfulness," "Listening to music"), modals for advice ("You should try," "People could consider"), and present continuous for trends ("People are practicing," "More individuals are discovering"). It also incorporates the target vocabulary nouns, verbs, and adjectives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Infer opinions in a simple presentation or lecture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the presentation once to get the main idea. The teacher asks: "What is the main topic of this presentation?" "What are the two main ways mentioned to manage stress?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Specific information):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise or fill-in-the-blanks with key words from the target vocabulary.
                        <p style="margin-left: 15px; margin-top: 5px; text-align: justify;">Example:
                            1. Forest bathing is about spending time outdoors to reduce ______. (stress) (True/False or fill in the blank)
                            2. The speaker thinks ______ is a traditional way to improve focus. (meditation) (True/False or fill in the blank)
                            3. Listening to ______ is presented as a helpful technique for relaxation. (music) (True/False or fill in the blank)</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy-focused activities for listening comprehension and language use, integrating target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Inferring opinions and identifying relaxation techniques. Using gerunds, modals for advice, and present continuous for trends. Applying vocabulary: <i>breathing, relaxation, forest bathing, mindfulness, meditation, stress, wellness, nature, focus, music, exercise, try, suggest, encourage, relieve, manage, relaxing, mindful, deep, soothing, beneficial, calming, effective, peaceful, serene, stressful, overwhelmed</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening for Opinions and Gerunds):</b> Students listen to specific segments of the audio again. The teacher pauses the recording and asks students to identify the speaker's opinion or advice. For example: "What does the speaker think about <i>practicing mindfulness</i> for improving focus?" or "Which sentence shows the speaker <i>suggesting deep breathing</i> for relaxation?" Students note down the gerunds and modals used to express opinions or give advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion and Modals):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings related to stress management and the audio content. Students complete them orally in pairs, using modals for advice (<i>should, could, must</i>) and target vocabulary.
                        <p style="margin-left: 15px; margin-top: 5px; text-align: justify;">Example:
                            - "To reduce stress, you ______ try..." (e.g., "you should try <b>forest bathing</b>.")
                            - "For better focus, people ______ consider..." (e.g., "people could consider <b>practicing mindfulness</b>.")
                            - "If you feel <b>overwhelmed</b>, you ______ listen to..." (e.g., "you must listen to <b>calming music</b>.")</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Identifying Trends - Present Continuous):</b> In pairs, students discuss statements from the audio that describe trends using the present continuous. They identify if the speaker implied something is popular or trending.
                        <p style="margin-left: 15px; margin-top: 5px; text-align: justify;">Example:
                            - Teacher: "The speaker said, 'Many people are discovering the benefits of spending time in nature.' What trend is this?"
                            - Student A: "It means <b>people are practicing forest bathing</b> more."
                            - Student B: "Yes, it shows that this technique <b>is becoming popular</b> for relaxation."
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language actively in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion - "Wellness Strategies for Students"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form small groups (3-4 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is given a prompt: "Based on the presentation and your own experiences, discuss how high school students can effectively manage stress using natural techniques or 'biohacks'. Share your opinions and give advice to each other."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must actively listen to each other's opinions, infer the speaker's viewpoint, and share their own. They are encouraged to use:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target vocabulary (e.g., <i>mindfulness, relaxation, deep breathing, exercise, nature, focus, soothing, beneficial, overwhelmed</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gerunds (e.g., "<i>Practicing meditation</i> helps me focus.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Modals for advice (e.g., "You <i>should try spending</i> time in a <i>quiet spot</i> like Parque Omar for better <i>relaxation</i>.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Present continuous for trends (e.g., "I think <i>more students are trying deep breathing</i> techniques these days.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pragmatic functions: Giving advice politely ("You <i>could try</i> relaxation techniques like deep breathing."), sharing personal wellness strategies ("In my experience, <i>listening to music</i> helps with focus.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sociolinguistic functions: Asking clarifying questions to understand better others' opinions (e.g., "Why are more students practicing mindfulness these days?" "Could you explain what you mean by 'sensory relaxation'?")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing participation and providing support as needed.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to gauge learning outcomes and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group discussion, focusing on active listening, inferring opinions, and participation in sharing ideas and advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group discussion, demonstrating their ability to infer opinions from the audio and share their own, using the target language. The teacher will listen for:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>mindfulness, soothing, beneficial</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of gerunds and modals for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and intelligibility of pronunciation (including /uː/ and reduced vowels).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to infer and discuss opinions expressed in the lecture and by peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asking clarifying questions respectfully.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences at the end of the lesson:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned that __________________ can help manage stress because __________________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase I can use to give advice on relaxation is __________________."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, informing future instruction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the presentation was most challenging for you (e.g., understanding specific words, inferring opinions, keeping up with the speed)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now in discussing different ways to manage stress and giving advice to others?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The warm-up effectively activated prior knowledge, and the audio input was appropriate for B1.2. Students showed good engagement in identifying main ideas and specific details. The group discussion allowed for good practice of sharing opinions and giving advice, particularly with modals and gerunds. Some students needed prompting to ask clarifying questions during the discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments needed: Provide more explicit sentence starters for asking clarifying questions in future listening tasks. Ensure a wider range of students participate in the whole-class sharing of opinions. Scaffold the pronunciation practice for multisyllabic words more intensely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: This lesson introduced key vocabulary and concepts related to stress management and biohacks through listening. In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a text that further elaborates on "Forest Bathing and Other Biohacks" using the same vocabulary. This will reinforce their understanding of the context and prepare them for deeper engagement with the theme.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>